<GuideArticle slug="three-days-in-oranjestad-on-foot"
  title="Three days in Oranjestad, on foot"
  metaTitle="Three Days in Oranjestad on Foot — The Walking Itinerary | Aruba Guide"
  description="A complete three-day Aruba itinerary that never starts a car: the pastel waterfront, Surfside swims, Eagle Beach by bus, Fort Zoutman, the tram, and where to eat every night."
  kicker="The flagship itinerary">
  <p>This is the itinerary we send guests, unabridged. It assumes you're sleeping downtown and treats the city as the point, not the compromise.</p>
  <h2>Day one — the waterfront and the old town</h2>
  <p><strong>Morning:</strong> coffee, then walk the harbourfront north past the cruise terminal — the pastel Dutch gables you came for are best photographed before 10 AM light. Loop back along Main Street on the <strong>free streetcar</strong> (the Caribbean's only one) to the plaza. <strong>Afternoon:</strong> Fort Zoutman — Aruba's oldest building, 1798 — and the small, good Historical Museum. <strong>Evening:</strong> dinner on the waterfront; Oranjestad's restaurant quarter is the island's largest spend category for a reason. Order the keshi yena once this trip — a stuffed-cheese Aruban classic.</p>
  <h2>Day two — beach day, two speeds</h2>
  <p><strong>Morning:</strong> walk 15 minutes south along Linear Park to <strong>Surfside Beach</strong> for a calm swim before the heat, planes drifting overhead into AUA. <strong>Afternoon:</strong> bus or $10 taxi to <strong>Eagle Beach</strong> — ten minutes, and you're on one of the Caribbean's ranked-best beaches; find a fofoti tree, stay for the famous low-crowd sunset. <strong>Evening:</strong> back in town, make it a short walk: the Renaissance Marketplace side for harbourside tables.</p>
  <h2>Day three — the water or the wild coast</h2>
  <p><strong>Option A, the catamaran:</strong> morning snorkel sails leave from the Oranjestad harbour area — the Antilla wreck and Boca Catalina are the stops that earn the trip. <strong>Option B, the north coast:</strong> the one day a vehicle earns its keep; a half-day UTV or jeep tour from town covers the Alto Vista chapel, the Bushiribana gold ruins and the natural bridge coastline. <strong>Final evening:</strong> sunset from the pool deck, then the five-minute question of which of the thirty restaurants within reach you haven't tried yet.</p>
  <h2>The practical paragraph</h2>
  <p>Everything above is in US dollars (Aruba's effective currency), tap water is excellent (desalinated, among the world's cleanest), the island is outside the hurricane belt, and English, Spanish, Dutch and Papiamento are all spoken everywhere. February–April is peak; September–November is the quiet, warm bargain.</p>
